commit 86324b3b4fa658b7d37cf813732f2231e19605fb
Date: 2026-06-25 18:47:07 +0000

Harden blob-backup SLURM array so all 16 tasks run and are verified

Array tasks 10-15 (which map to storage partition 002) were silently not
running, so nothing was ever written to /orcd/data/dandi/002/... The routing
logic in _dandi.py and BLOBS_HEAD_TO_PARTITION are correct; the failure was in
the SLURM/deployment layer.

Root cause: the job ran on mit_preemptable without --requeue. SLURM schedules
array tasks in ascending index order, so the high-index tasks (10-15) are
placed last; on the contended preemptable partition they either never won a
slot or were preempted and killed outright (no requeue). The old per-task
`flock -n <cmd>` form then skipped work silently, and there was no per-task
logging, so the loss was invisible. The outer cron-level flock only guards
sbatch submission and is not implicated.

Changes:
- backup_blobs_batch_deployment.sh: add --requeue so preempted tasks come back;
  add per-task --output/--error logs; acquire the per-task lock on an fd and
  FAIL LOUDLY (logged error + non-zero exit) instead of silently skipping;
  propagate the s3backup exit code. Quote SLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID throughout.
- Add `s3backup dandi verify-blobs` and verify_blobs_backup_deployment.sh: a
  post-run guard that checks both partitions received writes and alerts (exit
  non-zero) if any partition got zero sub-directories, which would have caught
  this bug.
- README: chain the verify job after the array via --dependency=afterany,
  document the one-time logs directory setup, and note the manual check.

